Now let me think about how to trigger the zero-length buffer in the hash function. The `drawing_hash` uses `STRING(text)` which calls `fnv_32a_buf(p->member.str, p->member.len, hval)`. If `text.len` is 0, this crashes.

The `clip_drawing_text` is set from `parse_vector_clip`, which gets text from args. The args are pushed by `push_arg` which only pushes if `end > start`. So the text is non-empty.

But wait - let me look at the `\p` tag and how drawing text is handled. In `parse_events`, when `drawing_scale` is set, the drawing text is captured. Let me re-examine:

```c
} else if (render_priv->state.drawing_scale) {
    q = p;
    if (*p == '{')
        q++;
    while ((*q != '{') && (*q != 0))
        q++;
    drawing_text.str = p;
    drawing_text.len = q - p;
    code = 0xfffc; // object replacement character
    p = q;
    break;
}
```

If `*p == '{'`, then `q = p + 1`, so `drawing_text.len = 1`. But wait, if `*p == '{'`, then `q = p + 1`, and the while loop checks `*q != '{' && *q != 0`. If `*q == '}'`, then the loop stops, and `drawing_text.len = q - p = 1`. So `drawing_text.str` points to `{` and `len = 1`.

Hmm, but what if `*p` is `{` and `*q` (which is `p+1`) is `}`? Then `drawing_text.len = 1`. That's non-zero.

Let me think about the font hash path instead. The `font_hash` uses `STRING(family)`. The family is set from the `\fn` tag or from the style's FontName. Let me look at how the style's FontName is set.

